context_budget.rs:161-167 — the None condition: `if keep_from == 0 || batches <= 1 { return None; }`. So compaction refuses when:
- `batches <= 1`: the kept side is one batch — i.e., the most recent exchange alone eats the entire recent-turns budget (16.6k chars here), OR
- `keep_from == 0`: everything fits within the recent budget — nothing to summarize.

In the failed run: the recent turns included big tool results; the character budget = (200000/4)×(1/3) ≈ 16,666 chars. The newest batch = assistant(fetch,fetch with args) + 2 tool results (~8,032 + ~3,475... wait the LAST 5 messages: tool(diff 7995), tool(8032), assistant(fetch,fetch), tool(8032), tool(3475)). Hmm — batching: the assistant with tool_calls opens a batch; its tool messages belong to it. So the newest batch = assistant(fetch,fetch) + tool(8032) + tool(3475) ≈ 11.5k chars. The one before: the assistant whose tool_calls... the 2nd-from-last batch = [tool(7995), tool(8032)] answers an EARLIER assistant's calls (before the shown window). Each batch ~8-11.5k chars > 16.6k? No — 11.5k < 16.6k, so the first batch is kept (batches=1), then adding the next batch (8k) → 19.5k > 16.6k → break. batches=1 → **`batches <= 1` → None**. EXACTLY the observed death: "the recent turns already fill the context budget".

So the dead-end is precise: when the most recent ONE-TO-TWO exchanges exceed a third of the context budget in characters, compaction refuses and the run dies — REGARDLESS of how much earlier history could have been summarized. The issue's proposal ("最古の側を落としつつ直近を維持") — the code ALREADY keeps the newest batch even when it alone exceeds the budget (lines 150-152); the refusal is when `batches <= 1` — i.e., keeping one batch means dropping NOTHING... wait no: batches counts KEPT batches. `keep_from` starts at len() (keep nothing), and the loop adds batches NEWEST-FIRST while they fit... first batch kept unconditionally (batches=1, keep_from=start). Then it tries older batches; when budget exceeded → break. None when batches<=1: the loop broke after the FIRST batch (the newest exchange alone fills the budget) — so a compaction would keep exactly one batch and drop the rest... and `batches <= 1` returns None ANYWAY?! Wait — that reads odd: "A compaction must DROP at least one whole batch" — keeping 1 batch of many WOULD drop many batches. But `batches <= 1` → None. So the refusal is: the kept side (1 batch) already fills the recent budget, so summary+1 batch ≈ the same prompt that just failed → the next turn exceeds again → pointless. Hmm — right: the SUMMARY itself costs tokens (the summary inference's output becomes part of the next prompt — summary + kept turns must fit under the limit). If the newest batch alone ≈ the whole budget in chars... no wait, the newest batch was 11.5k chars ≈ 2.9k tokens — the summary would be ~1-2k tokens; total ~5k tokens << 200k. That would FIT!

Hmm wait — that doesn't add up. Let me recompute: budget_chars = limit/4 × 1/3 = 200000/4×(1/3) = 50000×0.333 = 16,666 chars. The newest batch (assistant+2 tool results) ≈ 11.5k chars ≤ 16,666 → kept (batches=1). Next older batch: the messages BEFORE the shown 5: tool(7995) + tool(8032) — but these two tools belong to an EARLIER assistant (batch start walks back to the assistant whose tool_calls they answer — NOT in the shown window; that assistant + its 2 tool results ≈ maybe 8.5k chars) → 11.5k + 8.5k = 20k > 16.6k → break. batches=1 → None.

So the refusal fired because the newest EXCHANGE + the summary would... hmm, the comment says: "a session whose recent turns already fill the whole budget is one enormous message away from the same limit, and a summary would be paid for a session the next turn exceeds again". The DESIGN assumption: keeping 1/3 of the budget in recent turns + summary < limit. When the newest batch ALONE ≥ 1/3 budget... the design treats "recent turns fill the whole budget" loosely via batches<=1. In our case the newest batch is 11.5k chars (~2.9k tokens) — a summary (~1k tokens) + 2.9k ≈ 4k tokens — WAY under 200k!! The compaction would have WORKED. The refusal is far more conservative than necessary: `batches <= 1` rejects ANY keep of a single batch, even a tiny one.

Hold on — wait. Is that right? Re-read the loop:

```
let mut keep_from = messages.len();  // keep nothing yet
let mut kept_chars = 0;
let mut batches = 0;
while keep_from > 0 {
    let start = batch_start(messages, keep_from - 1);
    let batch_chars = sum(messages[start..keep_from]);
    if batches > 0 && kept_chars + batch_chars > budget_chars { break; }  // only checks budget from the SECOND batch on
    keep_from = start;
    kept_chars += batch_chars;
    batches += 1;
}
if keep_from == 0 || batches <= 1 { return None; }
```

So: first iteration: batches==0 → no budget check → keep the newest batch unconditionally, batches=1. Second iteration: budget check → exceeded → break with batches=1 → None.

Hmm — so with the observed session, batches=1 → None → death. BUT WHY does batches<=1 refuse? The doc: "A compaction must DROP at least one whole batch, and what it drops has to be worth summarizing" — keeping 1 batch and dropping 150 messages WOULD drop plenty... The condition `batches <= 1` returns None when only ONE batch was kept — hmm, that means it refuses whenever the newest batch + budget... no — it refuses whenever the SECOND batch didn't fit. That's the case here.

WAIT — that seems like a genuine design flaw for this shape: the recent-turns budget (1/3 of limit in CHARS at 4 chars/token) vs the actual token cost. The 4-chars-per-token estimate is calibrated for... Japanese text is ~1-4 chars/token (kanji ≈ 1-2 chars/token!) — JSON tool results ≈ 3-4 chars/token. The session's REAL prompt was 200,430 TOKENS = ~800KB chars (matches the 785,800-byte session file!). The recent-turns budget in chars = 16,666 chars ≈ 4k tokens. So the design keeps recent turns to ~2% of the limit in tokens, and the summary gets... hmm wait — that's the character budget for the KEPT TURNS; the summary is unbounded (model output).

So the refusal `batches <= 1`: the newest exchange (11.5k chars ≈ 2.9k tokens) is kept; the summary would cover the other 152 messages (~773KB chars ≈ 193k tokens summarized to maybe 2-5k tokens). Summary (3k) + kept (3k) ≈ 6k tokens — FITS EASILY. The compaction SHOULD have run! The `batches <= 1` refusal seems overly strict — it refused a compaction that would have rescued the session.

Hmm — wait, unless I'm misreading the None's intent: "a summary would be paid for a session the next turn exceeds again" — the SUMMARY inference itself: to generate the summary, the core sends the WHOLE conversation to the model with SUMMARY_PROMPT — **that request is 200,430 tokens > 200,000 limit → the summary inference ITSELF would 400!** THAT's the real dead-end: you cannot summarize a conversation that no longer fits the window — the summarizing request IS the request that fails. The compact() None for batches<=1 is a heuristic for "the kept side alone ≈ budget"; but the DEEPER dead-end is: once prompt > limit, the summary inference cannot run either (the summary request carries the full history!). Hmm — wait, does the summary request carry the full history? "The caller has already produced `summary` (one inference with SUMMARY_PROMPT)" — the summarizing inference sends the conversation. If the conversation doesn't fit the window, the summary request fails → that's the OTHER arm: "or the summarizing inference itself failed" (execution.rs:136-137).

So which arm fired here? The run log had "WARN 2行" — execution.rs:658 `tracing::warn!("{}", e)` prints compact_session's error. What does compact_session warn on the None path? Let me check compact_session (execution.rs) — the None → error message. The observed log (from the issue): `the recent turns already fill the context budget, so there is nothing to compact away` → then `Session exceeded its context limit (200430 of 200000 tokens) and could not be compacted`. So the FIRST warn is the None path ("nothing to compact away") — i.e., compact() returned None (the batches<=1 arm), NOT a summary-inference failure. So the None arm fired.

Was the summary inference even attempted? Per the flow: `compact_session` — does it run the summary FIRST and then compact() on the result? The issue's log order suggests the None check happened — if the None check happens BEFORE the summary inference, then no summary attempt was made. Let me verify in execution.rs's compact_session — need to read it. This matters for the "what should the fix do" recommendation: if the summary could NOT have run (request over limit), the correct escape is different (e.g., drop-then-summarize: first trim the oldest messages WITHOUT a summary — losing them — then continue; or end with a precise recover instruction). If the summary COULD have run (only the character-heuristic refused), the fix is the heuristic.

